WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.611
If you ever had to record an interview or a podcast

00:00:02.611 --> 00:00:05.120
between multiple people with just one microphone,

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.840
you know the pain of trying to edit that into something listenable.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:11.360
I'm Sarah, and as someone who has dealt with that exact situation,

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.020
I still have nightmares about working

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.040
with all that crosstalk and background noise.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.285
But now with Adobe's enhanced audio features,

00:00:17.285 --> 00:00:19.680
separating multiple voices on a single recording

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.120
into separate editable tracks can be accomplished with the click of a button.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.336
I'm going to show you how to not only do this,

00:00:25.336 --> 00:00:27.600
but also how to automatically clean up dialogue

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.773
and create contextual sound effects and ambiance,

00:00:29.773 --> 00:00:31.680
all without leaving your Premiere timeline.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:36.960
Today we're working on a sample podcast episode that was recorded with one microphone

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.910
in the same room with lots of variation

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:40.960
in audio volume between the two speakers.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.240
Hello and welcome to our podcast about Adobe Premiere audio tools.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:47.440
I'm Sarah.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:48.480
And I'm Lauren.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.504
And we're really excited to talk to you about some new audio tools

00:00:51.504 --> 00:00:53.200
that are coming straight to Premiere.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.240
Let's clarify, you're going to be talking about them because you're the expert.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:56.880
That is true.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.200
I'm just here to listen and absorb all the information.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.200
First things first.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.480
Let's get the audio track separated out so we can start editing.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:08.880
With the audio selected on the timeline, we're going to go up here to the properties panel and

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.480
select Separate Crosstalk.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.880
A dialog box will appear informing us how many credits this will cost us.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.520
We'll go ahead and click Separate.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.760
Premiere will now create the separated clips for us.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.613
Three different clips will be created, one for each speaker

00:01:23.613 --> 00:01:25.280
and one for any background sounds.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:29.360
Once the process is finished, we can work with the clips as normal.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:31.840
And then we're recording it in this case. Yeah.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:35.280
I'm not filming because why? We don't need to.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.680
Nobody needs to see what we look like right now.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.400
If it's got video, it's not a podcast. That's how I feel.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.600
I mean, I agree. A lot of other people would maybe not.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:45.440
But anyway.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.531
The original clip will still be right here on the timeline, automatically muted but unchanged,

00:01:49.531 --> 00:01:50.880
in case we need it again later.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.025
Now, before we start trimming our clips down into what we want to use,

00:01:54.025 --> 00:01:56.720
we'll improve the sound quality with the Enhance Audio tool.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:00.000
With a clip selected, we're going to go back to the properties panel
